{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>A bride-price and a gift</b> — מֹהַר (mohar) is the ketubah, that is, the thing given to the virgin, as it is written (Exodus 22:16) \"according to the mohar of virgins,\" and these are the tokens that young men send to virgins when they betroth them. And מַתָּן (mattan) is gifts, or silver and gold, to her father and her brothers. And in Bereshit Rabbah (80:7) they said: <b>מֹהַר</b> — פְּרָאנוּן; \"מַתָּן\" — פְּרָא פְּרָנוּן; and they are ketubah and nikhsei melog in the Jerusalem dialect. That is to say, that he gives her from his own property assets that shall be hers as the assets of her father's house, and they shall be in his possession as nikhsei melog. And the reason for this enticement was so that they would give her to him as a wife willingly, for the young woman was not reconciled to him but cried and wept continually, and this is the meaning of \"and he spoke to the heart of the young woman.\" Therefore he said, \"Take me this young woman as a wife,\" for she was already in his house and in his hand, and he did not fear her brothers, for he was the prince of the land — and how could they tear her away from him to take her out of his house? And Shechem's great passion was because the young woman was very beautiful, but Scripture did not describe her beauty as it did with Sarah and Rebekah and Rachel, for it did not wish to mention her beauty, since being drawn to her was for him a stumbling-block of sin. And concerning the praise of righteous women Scripture speaks, and does not disgrace them. Likewise it did not mention what was done with her afterward. And according to the plain sense, she remained with her brothers bound in living widowhood, for she was defiled in their eyes, as it is written <b>who defiled Dinah their sister</b>. And our Sages disagreed concerning her (Bereshit Rabbah 80:11), and the closest to the truth are the words of the one who says: Simeon took her and buried her in the land of Canaan. And this is as we have said, that she was with him in his house as a widow, and went down with them to Egypt and there died, and was buried in the land, and her burial-place is known to this day by tradition — it is in the city of Arbela, at the grave of Nitai the Arbelite. And it is possible that Simeon brought up her bones out of compassion for her, or that Israel brought them up together with the bones of her brothers, all the tribes, as our Sages mentioned (Bereshit Rabbah 100:11):"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {0 "The Aramaic transliteration terms פְּרָאנוּן and פְּרָא פְּרָנוּן are rendered as found in the Midrash; they appear to distinguish between the ketubah payment and the additional gift."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:10.543302452Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}